  "subtitle": "The key design decisions that make Perry fast",
  "nanBoxing1": "Perry uses a 64-bit JavaScript value representation where dynamic values can carry numbers or tagged pointer-like payloads. Representation selection can keep proved values unboxed instead of forcing every value through the generic form.",
  "nanBoxing2": "Numbers use IEEE 754 representation where possible; strings, objects, closures, and other heap values use tagged encodings and GC-managed storage. The compiler and runtime cooperate to preserve JavaScript semantics at dynamic boundaries.",
  "monomorphization1": "Generics and known call shapes can be specialized at compile time. Perry also preserves dynamic fallbacks where the receiver or value type is not proved.",
  "monomorphization2": "Specialization can turn known numeric and object operations into direct machine operations, but TypeScript annotations are mostly erased and do not guarantee a concrete runtime representation by themselves.",
  "staticDispatch1": "Known receivers can use direct calls and inlining. Unknown or dynamic receivers use runtime method dispatch, prototype lookup, and class-vtable paths.",
  "staticDispatch2": "This hybrid model gives LLVM static optimization opportunities without claiming that every JavaScript method call is resolved at compile time.",
  "zeroCost1": "TypeScript types guide compilation, while Perry still links a runtime and garbage collector to implement JavaScript semantics, async work, objects, strings, and dynamic values.",
  "zeroCost2": "Interfaces and many annotations erase cleanly, but classes, dynamic dispatch, allocation, and GC can have runtime costs. Perry optimizes proved cases rather than promising zero-cost abstraction universally.",
